  "primary_no_body_probe": "Only the file-scope declaration changed to `static const char replay_header[6] = \"ITR140\";`; the create_replay function body source was untouched. Object emits `_replay_header` in .rdata at offset 0x330 with bytes ITR140. The report labels +44/+52 as unique-content resolutions, not named-owner resolution. Independent cross-check: candidate DWARF gives the static variable const char[6], candidate COFF owns the relocation target at .rdata+0x330, candidate initializer is all six bytes, and historical DWARF/COFF records the corresponding unique header owner/type/initializer/address. This supports the data-owner crosswalk, but do not treat the verifier's content-only label alone as proof.",
  "stronger_name_diagnostic": "A diagnostic variant renamed the C identifier and the one body reference to historical spelling REPLAY_HEADER. It proves the header relocation pair by independent DWARF/COFF ownership and initializer; that stronger owner crosswalk anchors candidate .rdata+0x330 to historical 0x4d7dd0. The remaining Harold reference is candidate .rdata+0x99; under the same section base it would target 0x4d7b39, while the historical operand is 0x4d7a7e. This variant changes one body token and is not a proposed repair; it exposes a conflicting same-section string-placement relationship.",
  "conclusion": "The current body-level mismatch is caused by a mutable 7-byte .data header declaration. A no-body-change declaration with exact constness/extent yields verifier FUNCTION_MATCH and preserves all baseline exact neighbors; however, its relocation-resolution labels use unique-content search. Independent candidate COFF/DWARF evidence identifies the six-byte header at .rdata+0x330. Anchoring that owner exposes an incompatible relative location for the 'Harold' literal in the same candidate .rdata section (+0x99 versus historical 0x4d7a7e; 187-byte distance conflict). The function is therefore not robustly promotion-ready until CU-level string ownership/placement is resolved.",
  "single_rdata_base_consistency": {
    "candidate_header": {
      "section": ".rdata",
      "offset_hex": "0x330",
      "historical_va": "0x4d7dd0"
    },
    "candidate_harold": {
      "section": ".rdata",
      "offset_hex": "0x99",
      "historical_va": "0x4d7a7e"
    },
    "candidate_relative_distance_header_minus_harold": "0x297 (663 bytes)",
    "historical_relative_distance_header_minus_harold": "0x352 (850 bytes)",
    "inconsistency": "0xbb (187 bytes)",
    "implication": "A single .rdata contribution base cannot map both candidate references to their historical addresses. The no-body FUNCTION_MATCH variant resolves the header and Harold references independently by unique-content search, which does not prove consistent ownership once the header symbol is anchored."
  }
